#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/id #uuid "283001f5-f9e4-5990-a900-318677a48601" :question/math-academy-id "e-3740" :question/problem "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "006fe48a-9c8e-568b-90f1-40b12dc338bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3741" :question/problem "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "99e07b9d-aaaa-54b6-9118-78df023261f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3745" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34c298e-5082-53ae-bbcc-9abd9d3deebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303131} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd39f9b-7cd6-5cbd-a639-497db45e5e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132} {:answer/id #uuid "d89c0caa-656e-59de-873e-5402af7b4273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303133} {:answer/id #uuid "81f1adcc-62d5-51f4-80b1-cd547bd48eca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303134} {:answer/id #uuid "df90ad81-7c50-537c-9708-3715bba0d2e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-1)$ and  $(1,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34c298e-5082-53ae-bbcc-9abd9d3deebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303131} :answer-field/id #uuid "197f6263-c641-5680-8632-5bff24d869ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd8f4bb2-7373-5980-915b-81559c92c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-114060" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eec5bffe-f37b-542a-b883-24981acf56b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303138} {:answer/id #uuid "830cf24c-0dfa-5396-817d-7d783a1a481a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303139} {:answer/id #uuid "7495b2c2-665b-517b-a60b-9c61ed3ea1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303140} {:answer/id #uuid "c14260ea-f10e-52ce-bcc9-bed40a408717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303141} {:answer/id #uuid "426ba1bd-081f-552d-a1fe-5c1a630db5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "426ba1bd-081f-552d-a1fe-5c1a630db5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303142} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a4d10c-c21d-558f-b943-31033714e15e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0161bc1-4160-5449-a401-29481660be7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval  $(1,2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(1,2)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffde426-a97f-5dd2-afa0-be7cd1c04dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293689} {:answer/id #uuid "57a153c2-4712-5abd-aeb8-86da041cec06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293690} {:answer/id #uuid "03d4f9be-e445-52f2-9e85-2b5fc15af3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293691} {:answer/id #uuid "66e76bc3-16ac-5dbd-8410-79bf932a02c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293692} {:answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693} :answer-field/id #uuid "71107757-ea07-51d8-90b3-bf50cdd589cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7af01e6f-dc86-5725-9b0b-63602f01e9af" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1c2967-fe66-5164-8106-1c48cf85e021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293703} {:answer/id #uuid "98153d91-ae04-593a-acd3-4afb60a74acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293704} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd670c-e59b-51e5-8b48-a6c89b0f0fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293705} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ab2773-bf4c-52c2-ba6c-89081c9bdd5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293706} {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f1e316f-78a1-538e-a241-00deb8b4f4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0fa5633-3d2b-5f5b-a938-ef10da4c0590" :question/math-academy-id "q-114277" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3eb5451-e205-5b35-9824-99c2456d20e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is concave up nowhere." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181772} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9edf5c-e2b2-520b-873a-4ffc4b24637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181773} {:answer/id #uuid "2381c639-8b21-5a6e-acfb-e7c3f88bfcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181774} {:answer/id #uuid "563bd63c-f259-5b3a-873b-3fba61a4d3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181775} {:answer/id #uuid "605d6def-7586-52c2-aec4-d59ad36ff1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c9edf5c-e2b2-520b-873a-4ffc4b24637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181773} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc982674-a9e2-58d9-96a3-23989b918771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181771}] :question/id #uuid "649986b4-08b2-5319-8f70-a6b70ebe2bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-22794" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x) = 2x - \\ln (1 + x)$ is concave up."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863515ff-8ffa-5b28-9838-02b3f2ee569d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183005} {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} {:answer/id #uuid "e526471f-734d-50c7-8111-39708dfcf1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- ∞,-4)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183007} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b7fc8b-8aa3-5225-8b1a-b1142bd31a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183008} {:answer/id #uuid "3f44e70e-9e92-5956-86ee-8ffee243e87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(4, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c59ab91-fe4f-538c-9b7e-7e96d0c2a5ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f627add3-7eef-5ed1-b10b-a3822778f5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31214" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only."}]}
